<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Mecklenburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1348 CE – 1701 CE · capital Schwerin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Baltic duchy of the Obotrite-descended Niklotings, raised to ducal rank in 1348, whose partitions shaped north-east Germany until 1701.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-mecklenburg">Niklotings</a>, descendants of the Obotrite prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niklot">Niklot</a>, had ruled the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Lordship of Mecklenburg</a> since the 12th century. In 1348 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Emperor Charles IV</a> raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-duke-of-mecklenburg">Albert II</a> and his brother John to dukes of the empire. Albert's son Albert became King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> in 1364, and the duchy later absorbed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-stargard">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Stargard</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-schwerin">County of Schwerin</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The dukes shared power with strong estates of knights and towns, including the Hanseatic ports of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-rostock">Rostock</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/swedish-wismar">Wismar</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-rostock">Rostock University</a> was founded in 1419. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> was formally adopted in 1549. The economy rested on grain grown on noble estates by increasingly bound peasants, and repeated partitions, notably the creation of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-gustrow">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Güstrow</a> in 1621, weakened the dukes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Wallenstein</a> held <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-mecklenburg">Mecklenburg</a> from 1628 to 1631. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> line died out in 1695, a succession dispute followed, settled by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Hamburg</a> Compromise of 1701, which divided the land into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-schwerin">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Schwerin</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 1348 CE – 1701 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.53%;width:7.02%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1348 CE – 1701 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Schwerin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Albert II, Henry V, Adolf Frederick I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Low German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mecklenburg-Schwerin (to 1701), Herzogtum Mecklenburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1524&amp;focus=duchy-of-mecklenburg" aria-label="Duchy of Mecklenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4934 335 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of 